 "notes": [
  {
   "id": "np9-01",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Comparison",
   "quote": "pari diligentia … ac fuerat in bello",
   "trans": "with the same energy as he had shown in war",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Peace is measured against war: Hannibal brings to politics the same energy he brought to the battlefield, so his talents are not only military.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np9-02",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Characterisation",
   "quote": "diligentia",
   "trans": "energy, care",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Diligentia means hard work and attention to detail, a quality Romans admired in a magistrate. Nepos presents Hannibal as a model official.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np9-03",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Climax",
   "quote": "non solum ut esset pecunia … sed etiam superesset",
   "trans": "not only that there was money … but also that some was left over",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Not only … but also builds to a climax: Hannibal finds enough to pay Rome and then more besides.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np9-04",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Polyptoton",
   "quote": "esset … superesset",
   "trans": "there was … there was left over",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The same verb returns with a prefix, so the step from enough to more than enough is heard in the words themselves.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np9-05",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Irony",
   "quote": "quae Romanis ex foedere penderetur",
   "trans": "to be paid to the Romans under the treaty",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Rome's greatest enemy now uses his skill to make sure Carthage keeps up its payments to Rome.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np9-06",
   "cat": "order",
   "label": "Juxtaposition",
   "quote": "Roma legati Carthaginem",
   "trans": "from Rome envoys to Carthage",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The envoys are placed between the two cities' names, so the reader sees Rome reaching directly into Carthage's affairs.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np9-07",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Participle",
   "quote": "hos Hannibal ratus sui exposcendi gratia missos",
   "trans": "Hannibal, thinking they had been sent to demand his surrender",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Ratus gives the reason as Hannibal's own belief. Nepos does not confirm it, so the reader is left to judge whether he was right.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np9-08",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Gerundive",
   "quote": "sui exposcendi gratia",
   "trans": "for the purpose of demanding him",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The gerundive of purpose spells out what Hannibal fears: being handed over to Rome, the fate he spends the rest of the Life escaping.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np9-09",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Fast pace",
   "quote": "priusquam iis senatus daretur, navem ascendit clam",
   "trans": "before they were given a hearing by the senate, he boarded a ship secretly",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Hannibal acts before the Romans can even speak, which shows his decisiveness and his distrust of his own city.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np9-10",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Vivid detail",
   "quote": "clam",
   "trans": "secretly",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The great general slips away in secret, a sad change from the commander of armies.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np9-11",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Word choice",
   "quote": "profugit",
   "trans": "he fled",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The prefix pro- gives a sense of flight far away. It is the first of several escapes in the second half of the Life.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np9-12",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Word choice",
   "quote": "Poeni",
   "trans": "the Carthaginians",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Nepos switches to Poeni, the usual Roman name for the Carthaginians, just as they turn on their own hero.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np9-13",
   "cat": "order",
   "label": "Delay",
   "quote": "Poeni naves duas, quae eum comprehenderent, si possent consequi, miserunt",
   "trans": "the Carthaginians sent two ships to arrest him, if they could catch him",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The verb is held back behind a purpose clause and a condition, so the hunt for Hannibal is described before we learn that the ships were actually sent.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np9-14",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Asyndeton",
   "quote": "miserunt, bona eius publicarunt, domum a fundamentis disiecerunt, ipsum exulem iudicarunt",
   "trans": "they sent, confiscated his property, demolished his house, declared him an exile",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Four actions follow one another with no connecting words, which makes Carthage's response sound swift and relentless.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np9-15",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Vivid detail",
   "quote": "domum a fundamentis disiecerunt",
   "trans": "they tore his house down from its foundations",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Destroying the house to its foundations wipes out Hannibal's place in the city, an act of hatred as much as of law.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np9-16",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Irony",
   "quote": "ipsum exulem iudicarunt",
   "trans": "they declared him an exile",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The man who has just filled the treasury is condemned by the city he served, which shows the jealousy Nepos blamed in chapter 1.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np9-17",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "Hannibal's reforms",
   "quote": "novis vectigalibus",
   "trans": "from new revenues",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Livy (33.46–47) says that as suffete Hannibal broke the power of the corrupt judges' council and exposed the embezzlement of public money, so that the indemnity could be paid without new taxes on ordinary citizens.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np9-18",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "The date",
   "quote": "M. Claudio L. Furio consulibus",
   "trans": "in the consulship of Marcus Claudius and Lucius Furius",
   "line": "",
   "body": "They were consuls in 196 BC. Hannibal's flight from Carthage is usually dated to 195 BC.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np9-19",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "Antiochus",
   "quote": "Antiochum",
   "trans": "Antiochus",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Antiochus III \"the Great\", Seleucid king from 222 to 187 BC, ruled from Syria an empire reaching to Iran. His ambitions in Asia Minor and Greece brought him into conflict with Rome. Hannibal joined him at Ephesus in 195 BC.",
   "exam": ""
  }
 ],
 "section": 9,
 "questions": [
  "Nepos describes Hannibal's work as a magistrate in a single sentence. What does that sentence suggest about his abilities beyond war?",
  "Was Hannibal right to flee before the Roman envoys were even heard? What does the Carthaginians' reaction suggest?",
  "How does this section illustrate the judgement in chapter 1 that \"multorum obtrectatio devicit unius virtutem\"?"
 ]
}